<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Aschaffenburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1803 CE – 1810 CE · capital Aschaffenburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mainz territory on the Main formed into a principality for the archchancellor Dalberg during the Napoleonic reorganisation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a> had been the second residence of the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> for centuries, with the large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannisburg-palace">Johannisburg</a> palace. When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-mainz">Electorate of Mainz</a> lost its left-bank lands, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Imperial Recess of 1803</a> formed its remaining territories on the Main and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spessart">Spessart</a> into the Principality of Aschaffenburg for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Karl Theodor von Dalberg</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a> was the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/archchancellors-electorate">Archchancellor's Electorate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Dalberg</a>'s main residence. It was Catholic and rural, with forestry in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spessart">Spessart</a>. Dalberg promoted education, founding a school of forestry and maintaining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> university's remnants as a Karlsuniversität in Aschaffenburg.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1810 the principality became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-frankfurt">Grand Duchy of Frankfurt</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> fall it was given to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a> in 1814, to which it still belongs.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-aschaffenburg.jpg" alt="Principality of Aschaffenburg" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: User:Sodacan, wheel from File:Coat of Arms of Ernest August, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg (1661-1692).svg · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_the_Electorate_of_Mainz.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1803 CE – 1810 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aschaffenburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Karl Theodor von Dalberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fürstentum Aschaffenburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1806&amp;focus=principality-of-aschaffenburg" aria-label="Principality of Aschaffenburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4890 439 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Aschaffenburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5210 695L5279 723M5210 695L5199 691"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5279 723h0M5199 691h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5210 695h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5210 695h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Aschaffenburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1806&amp;focus=principality-of-aschaffenburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
